@file d3Sea                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     = node 0 — the hail
Who art thou that rows so bold
towards the Boyne's surging mouth?
 % Cé thú féin a iomramaíonn go cróga
towards the Boyne's surging mouth?
% i dtreo béal borb na Bóinne?
                                                        * Name yourself    

% Ainmnigh tú féin
@easca playerName                                                                                               @note met_muireann                                                                                               > {playerName} is my name.                                                                                          
% {playerName} is ainm dom.
> Wind that carried me greet thee.                                                                   
% Beannacht na gaoithe a sheol mé ort.
< Stone of the shore greet thee, {playerName}.                                                                                                          
% Beannacht chloch na trá ort, a {playerName}.
< I am Muireann.

% Is mise Muireann.

* Give no name                                                                                                  % Ná tabhair ainm
@note withheld_name                                                                                             
> My quarrel is with my own name, too small yet for any song.                                                                                           
% Is lenam ainm féin mo chomhrac, atá ró-bheag fós d'aon laoi.
< Ab ea.
% Is that so.

* Row on
% Iomramh ar aghaidh
% Row on / Lean d'iomramh
@exit
@silent


= node 1 — whose shore

* Where am I?
% Cá bhfuil mé?
@note knows_fionnbarra
> What chieftain's shore is this?
% Trá cén taoisigh í seo?
< Thy little boat has reached the mouth of Bóinn the bright and winding.
% Tháinig do bháidín go béal na Bóinne ghlé ghroí.

< This shore belongs first to her.
% Is léisí an trá seo ar dtús.

< Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse is Chief of this grey headland
% Is é Fionnbarra mac Dubhloingse Taoiseach na rinn liath seo

< and of the three valleys that run north.
% agus ar na trí ghleann a théann ó thuaidh.

* Row on
% Iomramh ar aghaidh
% Lean d'iomramh
@exit
@silent

= node 2 — the chief
[[ short. ]]

* Who is Fionnbarra?
% Cé hé Fionnbarra?
> What kind of man is Chief Fionnbarra?
% Cén cineál duine é an Taoiseach Fionnbarra?
< Fionnbarra is not an unjust man.
% Ní fear héagórach é Fionnbarra.

< His hounds are many.
% Is iomaí cú atá aige.

< But his toll-man waits at the river with a face like an angry stone.
% Ach tá a fhear cána ag feitheamh ag an abhainn agus aghaidh mar carraig fheargach air.


* Row on
% Iomramh ar aghaidh
% Lean d'iomramh
@exit
@silent


= node 3 — the toll-man


* His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?

@when note met_muireann
@note has_druid_word
> His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?

< If Fionnbarra's toll-man should meet thee at the strand,

% Má chasann Oidhrán ort ar an gcladach,
< thou art to say that Muireann the Druid knows thy name.
% abair leis go bhfuil d'ainm ag Muireann an Draoi.

* His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?

@when note withheld_name
> His toll-man?
% A fhear cána?

< Such is how he does business:
% Mar seo a dhéanann sé gnó:

< whom he robs not, he delays.
% an té nach ngoidfidh sé uaidh, cuirfidh sé moill air.


* Row on
% Iomramh ar aghaidh
% Lean d'iomramh
@exit
@silent

= node 4 — where art thou bound
And where now does the curragh turn its nose?

* Tell her 
% Inis di
@note told_muireann_truth
> To the bog that drinks men whole, to Allen's sunken ground.
% Chun na móna ina chailtear fir, chuig talamh báite Almhain.

> Where the Fianna test their own.
% Áit a thriailfar na Fhian.


* Tell her little
% Inis beagán

@note evaded_muireann
> Ask the heron of the fen. She will know my story.
% Íarr ar chorr na móna. Is aici a bheidh mo scéal.
< Speak plainly. What business brings a stranger to these lands?
% Freagair go díreach mé. Cén gnó a thóg chuig na tailte seo thú?
> The wind does not explain itself to the standing stone
% Ní mhíníonn an ghaoth í féin don ghallán. 
< Some purpose drives thee, I think.
% Tá rún i do chroí dod brú ar aghaidh, sílim.

* Row on
% Fág
@exit
@silent

= node 5 — she answers the plain answer
@when note told_muireann_truth
Hast thou mastered the spear?
% An bhfuil an gha fhoghlamaíthe agat?
* Fionn will teach me
% Gheobhaidh mé léann ó Fhionn
< I know Fionn. Fionn the great one.
% Tá aithne agam ar Fionn. \n Fionn an gaiscíoch mór.

< Fionn keeps poetry before the blade.
% Is uaisle le Fionn an fhilíocht ná an slea.

* Row on
% Fág
@exit
@silent

= node 6 — she answers the careful answer
@when note evaded_muireann
Where then art thou bound?

* Say even less
% Abair níos lú fós

> There is a thing that waits, and it is not yet found.
% Tá rud ag feitheamh ort nach bhfuil feicthe fós.

< Go then.
% Imigh leat mar sin.

@exit
@silent

= node 7 — the road


* Which way?
% Cén treo?

@note knows_rath
> Which way, then?
% Cén treo, mar sin?

< Half a morning's walk northwest along the track. Thou wilst see the ringfort.

% Siúil siar ó thuaidh feadh an bhealaigh leath na maidine. Feichfaidh tú an rath.
< Thou wilt find food, shelter, the hospitality of Fionnbarra.
% Gheobhaidh tú bia, dídean, agus fáilte Fhionnbarra ánn.

> And after?
% Agus ina dhiaidh sin?

< That great dark place, with water the colour of old bronze.
% An áit mhór dhorcha sin, agus uisce uirthi ar dhath an t-sean-chré-umha.
< It is not far. Two days west, two days south. Keep the hills to thy left hand.
% Níl sé i bhfad i gcéin atá sé. Dhá lá siar, dhá lá ó dheas. Coinnigh na cnoic ar do láimh chlé.

< May your journey succeed.
% Go n-éirí an bóthar leat.

* Row on
% fág

@exit
@silent

= node 8 — the parting

And if thou hear the sound of pipes where no one stands,
thou hast heard nothing. Walk on.
% Má thagann ceoil na bpíob chugat gan sheinteoir,  ná lig ort ach lean ort.

